# Features, targets, and backends

Treat the `upwell` facade feature list as a dependency graph, not as a platform promise. Server protocols are native surfaces; client support can retain generated types on Wasm while macros gate native hosting items out.

## Facade feature matrix

| Feature | Adds | Implies or requires |
|---|---|---|
| `di-check` | compile-time dependency graph checks | the only facade default feature |
| `daemon` | native RPC protocol and facade macros | `upwell-rpc` |
| `axum` | HTTP protocol, controllers, routes | `upwell-axum` |
| `ws` | generic WebSocket server/controller surface | `axum` |
| `json-ws` | JSON request/reply protocol | `ws` |
| `stomp` | STOMP messages and pub/sub | `ws` |
| `jobs` | scheduled jobs | protocol-neutral |
| `client` | generated client seams for enabled protocols | no transport by itself |
| `reqwest` | HTTP backend and browser fetch path | `client`; native and Wasm |
| `hyper` | pooled native HTTP backend | `client`; plain HTTP in the validated inventory |
| `tungstenite` | JSON-WS/STOMP WebSocket client transport | `client` + `ws`; native and Wasm runtime paths differ |
| `multipart` | Axum server extractor and generated body shape | `axum` |
| `openapi` | schema/operation collection and JSON mounting | `axum`; generated code requires direct `utoipa` |
| `openapi-*-ui` | one bundled UI | `openapi` |
| `yaml` | YAML and YML config parsers | TOML remains the config crate default |
| `watch` | filesystem-driven config reload | native filesystem watcher |
| `hybrid-registry` | explicit hybrid registration backend selection | advanced target/linker qualification |
| `wasm-ts` | newer generated TypeScript ABI mode | compatible direct `tsify` version |

The committed facade inventory also includes `uuid` support for templated STOMP topics and `tracing-subscriber` helpers. CLI/tooling behavior is compiled into the native framework/Cargo Upwell surfaces at this revision; there are no public facade features named `cli` or `tooling` in the committed manifest.

## Target matrix

| Surface | Native | Browser Wasm | Mach-O note |
|---|---|---|---|
| Build and serve `App<P>` | yes | no native socket/process host | an Apple host auto-selects `inventory`; cross-compilation is explicit |
| HTTP generated client | Reqwest or Hyper | Reqwest/fetch path | same generated controller definition |
| RPC TCP/Unix client | yes | no | Unix sockets remain Unix-only |
| JSON-WS request/reply | `TokioTungsteniteWs<P>` | `connectJsonWs` attach and `disconnectJsonWs` detach with `ws` + `json-ws` + `tungstenite` | do not assume an HTTP client implies a WS transport |
| STOMP client | native transport | browser `Connection` attach/connect API when generated | browser handshake cannot set arbitrary HTTP headers |
| Typed application directories | XDG, macOS Application Support, or Windows Known Folder paths | unavailable | exact locations are in the directory guide |
| Signals and file watching | yes where OS supports them | unavailable | SIGHUP is Unix-only |
| Link-time descriptor discovery | `linkme` normally | server groups are gated; client code remains | `inventory` is automatic on an Apple proc-macro host; force for cross-compilation |

<Callout type="warning" title="Do not infer a release contract from the label">

The 1.0.0 docs artifact records source package version 0.20.0 and a dirty Git snapshot. The matrices above describe validated public symbols and feature edges in that artifact. Confirm a published crate's `Cargo.toml` before pinning exact versions or relying on a target-specific generated shape.

</Callout>

## Add direct dependencies generated code names

Generated paths resolve in the application crate, so a transitive dependency is insufficient:

| Generated surface | Typical direct dependency |
|---|---|
| host entry/runtime | `tokio` |
| CLI host | `clap` |
| wire DTOs | `serde` |
| OpenAPI macros and schema | `utoipa` |
| browser bindings | `wasm-bindgen`, `wasm-bindgen-futures`, `tsify` |

Use target-specific dependency tables to keep server-only dependencies out of Wasm. See <GuideRef slug="clients/browser/setup-and-http">browser Wasm clients</GuideRef> for a complete manifest shape.

## Select the registration backend

Upwell emits exactly one registration implementation at macro expansion:

- `linkme` is the normal native backend and uses per-type distributed linker sections.
- `inventory` uses shared constructor-backed collections and avoids Mach-O's approximately 255 custom-sections-per-segment limit.
- An Apple/Mach-O proc-macro host automatically enables `upwell_hybrid`, so ordinary native macOS builds select `inventory` with no feature.
- Cross-compiling to an Apple target from a non-Apple host cannot infer the target at proc-macro expansion. Enable `hybrid-registry` or set `UPWELL_HYBRID=1` so generated code uses `inventory`.
- `UPWELL_HYBRID=0`, `false`, `off`, `no`, or an empty value explicitly restores `linkme` on an Apple host, provided `hybrid-registry` is not also enabled.

The Cargo feature only adds the inventory choice; it does not disable an environment-forced choice. Do not manually register descriptors: code generation emits one backend and `auto_discover()` consumes it through a uniform registry surface.

## Keep one protocol invariant

Cargo features may compile several protocol crates, but <code>App&lt;P&gt;</code> has exactly one selected <Symbol path="upwell::ProtocolDefinition" />. Enabling `daemon` and `axum` does not turn one application value into a mixed dispatcher.

```rust plain
let rpc = upwell::daemon::App::builder("internal-rpc")
    .auto_discover()
    .build()
    .await?;

let http = upwell::axum::App::builder("public-http")
    .auto_discover()
    .build()
    .await?;
```

These are separate hosts with separate lifecycle and protocol state. A supervisor can run them side by side, but shared infrastructure must be explicit and each app receives only descriptors appropriate to its protocol.

## Troubleshoot feature failures

- A generated client type missing usually means `client` or the protocol feature was absent when the defining crate compiled.
- A client method missing can be a backend capability constraint, especially streamed request bodies.
- `utoipa` unresolved under `openapi` means it is not a direct application dependency.
- Wasm failures mentioning native sockets, directories, or process signals indicate server bootstrap code leaked into the library target.
- A controller exists but no endpoint serves it when the protocol was not mounted or the wrong `App<P>` host collected it.
- Duplicate registration or linker behavior on a special native target should be diagnosed through the selected registration backend rather than fixed by manually calling every descriptor.

